# Design Systems & Tokens

## Stack default

Tailwind + a component library on top (shadcn/Radix). Don't reach for
plain hand-rolled CSS variables or a different utility framework unless the
project already committed to one — this is the default, not a per-project
decision.

## Immediately after scaffolding: reskin before shipping anything

Unmodified shadcn/Radix defaults (the default gray + violet/blue accent,
default radius, default font stack) read as generic AI-generated output.
Before writing any feature code:

1. Pick an accent color (ties to whatever mode `warisskill-ui-ux-visual-design-taste`
   selected — dense/internal can stay closer to neutral, bold/consumer
   should commit to something with actual character).
2. Pick a border-radius value and apply it project-wide via the theme
   config, not per-component.
3. Pick a font — system font stack is a valid choice, but make it a
   *choice*, not the leftover default.

This is not optional polish to defer — do it in the same pass as project
setup, before the first real screen is built.

## Token structure: two-tier, semantic aliasing primitives

Two layers, always:

- **Primitive tokens** — the raw scale (`--green-500`, `--gray-100`, etc.),
  source of truth for actual values.
- **Semantic tokens** — named by role, referencing primitives (`--success`,
  `--surface-primary`, `--border-subtle`). Components consume semantic
  tokens, never primitives directly.

This survives palette/rebrand changes without touching component code, and
makes dark-mode remapping (below) a token-file change instead of a
find-and-replace across components.

## Spacing scale

No personal override — Tailwind's default scale is the default. Don't
introduce a custom spacing scale unless a specific project's grid genuinely
requires it; that's added complexity with no established payoff here.

## Dark mode: day-one, not retrofitted

Every semantic token gets both a light and dark value from the start —
defined together in the same pass, not added later as a follow-up task.
When picking a component or writing a new one, verify both modes render
correctly before considering it done, the same way you'd verify a
responsive breakpoint.

## Limitations

- This is the web (Tailwind/shadcn-style) default. It does not apply to
  React Native/Expo, SwiftUI, Jetpack Compose, or desktop-native theming —
  those need their own platform-specific token approach.
- If a project arrives with an existing, different styling stack already
  established, follow it — this skill describes what to reach for on a
  blank slate, not a mandate to migrate existing projects.
